 ## Background
-The concept of weather and cloud parameterization in computer graphics emerged from the need to simulate realistic atmospheric effects in real-time applications. Early implementations relied on simple height-based density functions, but modern approaches integrate complex weather maps and procedural noise to create dynamic, large-scale cloud systems [S1, S2, S4, unofficial]. The development of efficient raymarching techniques and physically based shading models has allowed for more accurate representation of light scattering and self-shadowing in volumetric clouds [S3, S8, unofficial]. Key contributions include the integration of weather data into procedural noise generation [S1, S2, unofficial] and the use of multiple scattering octaves for improved visual fidelity [S3, unofficial].
+The concept of parameterizing weather for volumetric rendering originated in real-time graphics engines seeking to balance physical accuracy with performance. Early implementations relied on static height fields, but modern approaches integrate dynamic weather maps and multi-octave noise erosion [S1, S2, unofficial]. The specific parameter structures described in [S1, unofficial] and [S4, unofficial] reflect the evolution from simple shader uniforms to complex, serialized configuration files (e.g., RON) that drive both procedural generation and raymarching passes [S1, S4, unofficial]. The use of Henyey-Greenstein phase functions and Beer's Law variants has become standard for approximating light transport in participating media within these parameterized frameworks [S3, S4, unofficial].
 
